  "engagement_comment": "Name the longest red light in your town. We'll start: the one you're always late at. 🚦",
  "sources": [
    "Inductive-loop detector: turns of insulated wire in a sawed slot in the pavement, energized (about 10-200 kHz), connected by lead-in cable to an electronics unit in the controller cabinet: https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06108/02.cfm ; https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06139/appende.cfm",
    "A vehicle's metal induces eddy currents (in the undercarriage) that decrease the loop's inductance; the electronics unit senses the change and signals the controller: https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06108/02.cfm ; https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06139/appende.cfm",
    "The loop senses metal, not weight: https://www.ky3.com/2026/08/27/fact-finders-does-rocking-your-car-near-stop-signal-trigger-traffic-light/ ; https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06139/appende.cfm",
    "The inductive loop has become the most utilized sensor in traffic management; other signals use radar or video detection: https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/research/operations/its/06108/02.cfm ; https://www.ky3.com/2026/08/27/fact-finders-does-rocking-your-car-near-stop-signal-trigger-traffic-light/"
